WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.850
Bryan: If we were to actually get every kid
to graduate from college, you would need a

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:08.360
college degree to be a janitor.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:14.600
Nat: Bryan Caplan, welcome to AEI.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:15.600
Bryan: Thanks so much for having me

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.330
Nat: So your book, "The Case Against Education:
Why the Education System Is a Waste of Time

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:25.440
and Money" makes you sort of a popular guy,
maybe with an unpopular argument.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.650
How's the reception so far?

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.040
Bryan: Well, it's not that unpopular.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:31.690
So, maybe because the book's only 20 bucks
on Amazon I've been moving a lot of copies,

00:00:31.690 --> 00:00:34.510
and I've been getting some very fair reviews.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:37.839
People criticize me, but at least they criticize
me for what I said, so I've got no complaints.

00:00:37.839 --> 00:00:38.979
Nat: Okay, that's fair.

00:00:38.979 --> 00:00:40.949
All right, well, let's jump right in.

00:00:40.949 --> 00:00:48.370
So, you start off in the book and you talk
about sort of three things that education

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:51.159
communicates to employers, three perspectives.

00:00:51.159 --> 00:00:52.909
Can you sort of break those down for us?

00:00:52.909 --> 00:00:53.909
Bryan: Yeah, sure.

00:00:53.909 --> 00:00:55.489
I say it's a package of three big traits.

00:00:55.489 --> 00:00:59.299
So first of all, intelligence, such that your
education shows how smart you are.

00:00:59.299 --> 00:01:03.690
Secondly I say it also shows how hardworking
you are, because you could be really smart,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.250
but if you're really lazy you're not gonna
be able to finish high school, much less college.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:10.340
And then last and least obviously, I say it
signals conformity.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.399
It shows that you are willing to submit to
social expectations, that you're willing to

00:01:14.399 --> 00:01:16.170
be an obedient little sheep, right?

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.799
Which, of course, our society needs obedient
little sheep a lot of times, because if everyone

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:23.219
were a free thinker and free spirit, how would
anything get done?

00:01:23.219 --> 00:01:25.359
Nat: Hold on a second, you've got this all
wrong.

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:30.110
So, you know, I learned in graduate school
this human capital theory.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.190
Right, human capital theory?

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:35.000
We go to more years of schooling, and we get
more benefits.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.039
And that, you know, when I go out on a job
market, they're gonna say, "Yeah, that guy's

00:01:39.039 --> 00:01:42.020
got more years of school," and so that's why
we go to school?

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.189
Am I wrong?

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:45.490
Bryan: Well, what you've said isn't literally
wrong, but, you know...

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:49.360
So, the whole idea of human capital theory
is that education pays because school pours

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.370
useful skills into you.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.460
So you actually learn how to do your job better.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:53.460
Nat: You learn, right.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:55.070
Bryan: And I don't deny that occurs sometimes.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.110
So, literacy and numeracy are very useful
skills, and if you go to school to learn computer

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.270
programming or something like that, then you
really are emerging as a more skillful person

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.479
and it makes sense for employers to prefer
you.

00:02:05.479 --> 00:02:10.080
But I still say there's a lot of education
where they aren't teaching you very much,

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.920
or at least it's not very relevant to any
job you'd ever do, and yet employers still

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:13.920
seem to care.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.330
Like, why do employers care if you failed
your foreign language classes even though

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:20.510
the people who got A's don't speak the language
either?

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:21.510
Right?

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.280
And I say that reason is that when you do
well in school, you are sending a message

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.280
to employers.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.600
You're saying, "Look, I'm the kind of person
who has the package of brains, diligence,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.980
and conformity to go and jump through all
these hoops, so at least don't throw out my

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.530
application when I apply for the job."

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:42.360
Nat: Right, so in your book you say, let's
see, I have it in here, you say, "Signalling,"

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.760
which is this theory around signalling "has
been kept in the ghetto, and this book's goal

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.950
is to first emancipate it from the ghetto."

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:50.410
So why's it been in the ghetto?

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:54.650
Bryan: Right, so there are multiple Nobel
Prize winners that have worked on this idea

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:58.450
that part of what or why education pays is
just that you're impressing employers.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.320
You're getting certified, you're getting a
stamp on your forehead, you know.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.220
There's one economist Michael Spence, he really
got his Nobel prize entirely for this idea.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.560
But the strange thing is that people who look
at the real world, right, so in what we call

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.161
empirical research, those people who study
actual schools, actual education, the actual

00:03:12.161 --> 00:03:15.890
labor market, have never been very fond of
this idea.

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:19.880
And they treat it, yeah, yeah, sure there
is this idea but we're just gonna focus on

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.770
going and measuring the payoffs for education.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:25.611
And it really bugged me, because on the one
hand there's this explanation that seems to

00:03:25.611 --> 00:03:28.510
make a lot of sense, but the people looking
at the real world don't talk about it very

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:29.510
much.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:33.090
And I wanted to go and bring theory and reality
together, and that's really what this book

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:37.480
is all about, where I try to say, "Look, there's
actually a lot of empirical evidence, a lot

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:41.780
of evidence in the real world, that signalling
is a big part of what's going on in education,

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:43.710
it's a big part of the reason why it pays.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:47.040
And actually there's a lot of facts that you
really just can't explain without signalling.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.301
If you didn't believe in signalling, the world
would just make no sense at all."

00:03:49.301 --> 00:03:54.140
Nat: All right, so we have a human capital
theory which says kids are learning more and

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.250
that's why education's valued.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:59.610
And then we have a signalling theory that
says, you know, we have these sort of credentials

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.710
basically, it hinges on credentials.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:05.880
And those credentials are signals that folks
who are hiring in the labor market say, "Well,

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.910
you know, I see that signal so I know this
is quality."

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:14.870
And these aren't independent, and it seems
like your argument is certainly a step beyond

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:15.870
signalling.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.150
It's about empty signals.

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:18.150
Is that fair?

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:21.650
Bryan: Well, I mean, so, if you convince people
that you're great by learning a lot of useful

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:24.450
skills then human capital and signalling would
be almost the same thing.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:29.000
But one of the big puzzles that I talk about
in the book is, why do we spend so much time

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.820
learning subjects that almost no one uses
on the job?

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:36.120
Almost no one has a job that uses history,
or social science, or art, or music, or foreign

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.120
language.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.120
Right?

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:41.670
I mean, just think about all the classes that
you took where you never used them again after

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:43.100
the final exam.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.820
And yet employers care.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.260
If you were to say, "Look, I only did the
classes that I thought were useful, now give

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:51.560
me a job," most employers would not say, "Oh,
good thinking."

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.540
Instead they would not even be talking to
you because you application would be in the

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:55.540
trash.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.400
And signalling can explain what's going on
here, which is even when you study something

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:02.900
useless, something that you absolutely do
not need to know on the job, still the fact

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:05.690
that you did it and you jumped through these
hoops shows a lot of good things about you.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:10.030
And especially it shows that you are a conformist
person, that you went and followed the norms

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:11.370
of our society.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:15.470
You did what your parents, teachers, and peers
all said everything has to do.

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.690
Because if you're an employer do you wanna
hire someone who doesn't do those things?

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:19.690
I'd be nervous.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.570
I mean, I have a lot of nonconformist friends.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:22.889
I love them, they're fun.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:27.190
But if I were hiring people I would be scared
to hire those friends, I'd hire the boring

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:28.190
people.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:31.250
Nat: Yeah, I mean it seems like a safer bet
to hire somebody who's got the credential

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:33.030
than doesn't.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:38.780
But you also make some serious claims about,
you know, there's just a lot of waste.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:42.760
So paint this picture for us.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:46.750
Where's all this waste, and about how much
of schooling is wasted?

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:51.220
I mean, the subtitle of the book is "...The
Education System Is a Waste of Time and Money."

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.220
Bryan: That's right.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.220
Nat: Show me.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.850
Bryan: So my preferred number is 80% of the
payoff for education is signalling.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.460
And it's basically just a waste of social
resources.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.240
It doesn't mean it's a waste for the individual.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.840
The individual gets to look good and outshine
the competition.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:07.710
But is it the kind of thing that it makes
sense for taxpayers to encourage?

00:06:07.710 --> 00:06:08.960
And that's where I say no.

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.960
And there's a lot of different ways that you
can come at this, and I say all of these different

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:16.650
ways point in the same basic rough ballpark
of a number.

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:21.540
To me probably the most impressive evidence
is, let's take a look at the amount of education

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:25.510
that you need to get a job, not to do a job,
but to get a job, to be considered worthy

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:26.580
of employment.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:30.951
Since 1940, the amount of education that you
need to get one and the same job that your

00:06:30.951 --> 00:06:35.280
dad or grandfather had back then has risen
by about 3 years, almost a full degree.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.280
Right?

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:41.440
So in 1940, there'd be almost no waiters or
taxi drivers or cashiers with college degrees.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.520
It would have been almost unthinkable back
then.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.940
Now these are very common jobs for people
with college degrees to have.

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:48.020
All right?

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.090
And you say like, what's going on?

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:53.870
I say what's going on is that the more education
the workforce has, the more you need to impress

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:54.870
employers.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:59.260
Like, when almost nobody has even a high school
degree, then if you have a high school degree,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.860
employers say, "Look at this person, this
is management material."

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:05.180
Now no one looks and sees that you've finished
high school and says management material.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.510
Now you might need to have not only a college
degree, but an MBA.

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:10.270
So, you know, this is called credential inflation.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:14.860
It just means that, you know, like, aside
from the fact that jobs have changed...that's

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:15.860
happened too, right?

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.740
So there are some more intellectually demanding
jobs, but that only explains a small part

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:20.290
of what's going on.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:23.860
Most of what's gone on in the economy is that
you now need a lot more education in order

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:27.310
to be considered worthy of the very same jobs
that people used to have in the past.

00:07:27.310 --> 00:07:28.310
Nat: Right.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:31.430
So I hear a lot of folks saying, you know,
"We have this new economy.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:32.730
It's a new economy.

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:37.389
It's the future of economy, so kids are gonna
need more and better education, and newer

00:07:37.389 --> 00:07:38.389
education."

00:07:38.389 --> 00:07:39.389
Wrong?

00:07:39.389 --> 00:07:40.389
Right?

00:07:40.389 --> 00:07:43.540
Bryan: Well, it'd be nice if they were being
trained for the new economy, but I don't see

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:45.870
signs that schools are doing much of that
at all.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.830
So again, the education system is normally
extremely backwards-looking.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.800
Teachers basically teach you the same things
that they were taught when they were students.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.050
Their teachers did the same thing to them.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:56.080
If you look at the curriculum, the curriculum
has barely changed over time.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.980
There's a little bit more time for computer
science, although, you know, like, as far

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:03.770
as I've heard only Washington State lets students
do a computer language instead of a foreign

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.210
language, all right?

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:10.040
So, you know, there's almost no adaptation
to what the modern economy is, or is going

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:11.040
to be.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.280
Before, you know, you say, like, "Wow, like,
how can we even get by with this education

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:15.280
system?"

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.790
I say the answer is that almost all of the
actually useful job training happens on the

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:19.790
job.

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:23.620
The main thing that you do with your education
is not actually get ready to do a job.

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:27.160
The main thing you do is convince employers
that they could train you in the actual job

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.160
that they want you to do.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.160
Right?

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.300
And you know, of course you do learn some
useful things in school, and there are some

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:32.709
majors that are more vocational.

00:08:32.709 --> 00:08:37.639
So like, nursing, or medical school, or engineers,
or computer scientists, but it's always important

00:08:37.639 --> 00:08:42.519
to remember that these are exceptions and
that most students are doing majors in fields

00:08:42.519 --> 00:08:44.339
where there's almost no jobs at all.

00:08:44.339 --> 00:08:46.240
And then you're like, well, then where do
they get jobs?

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.130
They get jobs in something outside of their
fields.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:52.630
So, like, you know, every year we graduate
more new psychology BAs than there are jobs

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:53.870
in psychology.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:58.360
Every year we graduate more communications
BAs than there are jobs in the entire entertainment

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.370
industry.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:03.540
So it's not like people in psychology and
communications never get jobs.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:07.490
They just get a job where the employer says,
"I want someone with a college degree in something

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:09.259
or other," right?

00:09:09.259 --> 00:09:13.629
And the reason why the economy works is that
when you finally get that job, then the employer

00:09:13.629 --> 00:09:18.879
says, "Huh, well, did you learn how to do
videography while doing psychology?

00:09:18.879 --> 00:09:19.879
No?

00:09:19.879 --> 00:09:23.079
Well, that's unfortunate, but none of your
competitors know that either.

00:09:23.079 --> 00:09:24.079
Now let's go into it."

00:09:24.079 --> 00:09:26.769
Or maybe you did an internship where you did,
and then like, fine, you have an internship,

00:09:26.769 --> 00:09:27.769
that's useful actually.

00:09:27.769 --> 00:09:32.860
Nat: So, let me ask you this, does this system
work for employers, and does it work well?

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:38.670
I mean, if I'm on the hiring side, you know,
the demand side of the labor market, is it

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:39.670
working?

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:40.670
Bryan: Well, here's the main thing.

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:44.570
There's about a trillion dollars of government
money in favor of the status quo every single

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:45.570
year.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.629
So I wouldn't say the current system is passing
the market test by any stretch of the imagination.

00:09:49.629 --> 00:09:53.149
It's propped up by enormous subsidies, and
if government spent a lot less, there might

00:09:53.149 --> 00:09:56.800
be very different systems of figuring out
who's good for jobs, who's bad for jobs.

00:09:56.800 --> 00:10:00.220
There'd probably be a lot more apprenticeships,
there'd probably be a lot of other kinds of

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:01.220
certification.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:04.429
But at the same time, I don't see any sign
that employers are really looking around very

00:10:04.429 --> 00:10:05.480
hard for something else.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.589
You know, there are stories where people say,
"Well, employers wanna do other things but

00:10:09.589 --> 00:10:10.899
it's illegal."

00:10:10.899 --> 00:10:12.809
I did look into this idea in the book.

00:10:12.809 --> 00:10:16.639
Like for example, a lot of people say, "Employers
would do IQ testing for hiring if it were

00:10:16.639 --> 00:10:20.550
only legal, but since that's illegal they
do college instead."

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:24.580
So here's the striking thing, when I actually,
you know, did research on this, found out

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:32.889
that IQ testing is like 98% legal, 2% illegal,
98% legal, and the amount of actual punishment

00:10:32.889 --> 00:10:36.920
that you're likely to receive in the legal
system for doing IQ testing for hiring is

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:39.389
peanuts compared to the cost savings.

00:10:39.389 --> 00:10:44.360
So I have to say that I don't think that employers
are eager to switch to something else.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:48.869
And you know, my story for this is that if
you were to go and say we're hiring people

00:10:48.869 --> 00:10:52.889
that dropped out of high school but they have
high IQs, I think you would get a certain

00:10:52.889 --> 00:10:58.709
kind of really smart person, namely lazy,
non-conformist, defiant smart people who are...

00:10:58.709 --> 00:10:59.809
Again, I love them.

00:10:59.809 --> 00:11:02.629
A lot of these are my favorite people in the
world, but I don't wanna hire them.

00:11:02.629 --> 00:11:03.790
Nat: Yeah, you don't wanna depend on them.

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:05.120
Bryan: Yeah, I don't wanna depend on them.

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:06.120
I want, you know...

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:09.139
Well, I can depend upon them to be ready to
go to a concert on a moment's notice.

00:11:09.139 --> 00:11:10.139
Say, "Hey, buddy.

00:11:10.139 --> 00:11:12.190
Hey, smart, lazy friend, do you wanna go to
a concert?

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:13.190
Free tickets."

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:14.190
"Like, dude, I'm up for that."

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:15.329
So yeah, so those, you know, like...

00:11:15.329 --> 00:11:18.769
Nat: Depending on them to get your taxes done
by April 15th is a different question.

00:11:18.769 --> 00:11:19.769
Bryan: Exactly.

00:11:19.769 --> 00:11:20.839
Then, I want the boring person.

00:11:20.839 --> 00:11:21.980
Nat: Sure, I understand.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:23.369
So let me ask you a question.

00:11:23.369 --> 00:11:28.160
It's you're a little bit all over the map
here on which level of education you're talking

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:29.160
about.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:30.160
It sounds like you're talking about higher
education.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:34.019
And I know that a lot of people would say,
"Yeah, higher ed wastes a ton of money."

00:11:34.019 --> 00:11:35.920
So, but, you know, where does it stop?

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:39.820
I mean, high school certainly is always a
good investment, right?

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:43.759
Bryan: Well, for the individual, then generally
it pays very nicely.

00:11:43.759 --> 00:11:45.350
But you know, for society, I don't think so.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:48.319
So again, think about all of the time that
you wasted in high school, all the useless

00:11:48.319 --> 00:11:52.000
requirements, things that you had to do in
order to graduate that you're never going

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:53.000
to use again.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:56.550
I mean, it's just very striking if you make
an inventory of all the time that you spent

00:11:56.550 --> 00:12:00.439
in high school studying subjects that you
will never use after the final exam...

00:12:00.439 --> 00:12:04.139
You know, like, massive foreign language requirement,
two or three years, and yet almost no one

00:12:04.139 --> 00:12:08.029
in America learns to speak a foreign language
very well in school, all right?

00:12:08.029 --> 00:12:14.149
So you know, just like history, civics, art,
music, these are required classes to an insane

00:12:14.149 --> 00:12:18.019
extent, and yet if you don't jump through
these hoops, then you're not going to be allowed

00:12:18.019 --> 00:12:20.360
to advance, you're not gonna be allowed to
get into college, and you're cutting off a

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:21.360
lot of opportunities for yourself.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:25.009
So you know, like, one of the other things
that I say in the book is it'd be much better

00:12:25.009 --> 00:12:29.809
if kids, especially kids who just don't like
school...there are a lot kids like that.

00:12:29.809 --> 00:12:33.410
There are a lot of kids who are miserable,
bored out of their minds.

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:36.309
You know, if kids like this could instead
go and do vocational education, or they could

00:12:36.309 --> 00:12:39.180
do apprenticeships and be prepared for an
actual job...

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:42.459
You know, it's very striking that there are
countries like Germany and Switzerland where

00:12:42.459 --> 00:12:47.119
kids who just don't like school, never have,
when they're 13 or 14 or 15, they can go and

00:12:47.119 --> 00:12:48.300
do vocational education.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.300
Nat: But there's...

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:50.300
Bryan: Yes?

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:54.519
Nat: But we've got this educational credential
arms race.

00:12:54.519 --> 00:12:59.910
And with that background, right, for these
kids, it's not a rational decision for them,

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:00.910
right?

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:04.360
I mean, the rational decision for them would
be, look, if you drop out of high school,

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:06.439
things are not gonna go very well, am I wrong?

00:13:06.439 --> 00:13:09.279
Bryan: So, if you drop out of high school
and do nothing, then you're totally right.

00:13:09.279 --> 00:13:14.100
If you quit high school and learn to be a
plumber, especially if you're the kind of

00:13:14.100 --> 00:13:18.430
person who probably was gonna quit high school
anyway eventually, then I think there's actually

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.519
very good evidence that the vocational track
is a good idea for you.

00:13:21.519 --> 00:13:25.449
So the main idea in my book is just that taxpayers
are wasting a lot of money encouraging this

00:13:25.449 --> 00:13:26.709
rat race.

00:13:26.709 --> 00:13:32.389
But I do also have this side idea that even
in high school, there are a lot of kids, maybe

00:13:32.389 --> 00:13:36.720
even a third of kids or half of kids, who
would actually be even personally better off

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:40.220
if parents would just listen to them, and
teachers listen to them, and say, "Look, you

00:13:40.220 --> 00:13:41.600
don't wanna do college.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:45.989
College is not the kind of thing that in any
way inspires you.

00:13:45.989 --> 00:13:47.670
How about we go and teach you something else?"

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:50.970
And especially, you know, like, you have people
say, "Well, so you get tagged as a plumber

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:51.970
at 13?"

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:52.970
I say, "How about this?

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:56.800
How about when you're 13, if you don't like
school, we introduce you to 20 different occupations,

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:59.439
2 weeks as a plumber, 2 weeks as an electrician?

00:13:59.439 --> 00:14:01.100
Just try a bunch of things.

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:03.089
Is there anything you like and are good at
at all?"

00:14:03.089 --> 00:14:04.089
right?

00:14:04.089 --> 00:14:06.860
And I think there's a lot of 13-year-olds
who would jump at that chance because, you

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:10.480
know, like, they've spent years of their life
just being told, like, you're a C or a D student,

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:12.019
you're not good at this stuff, you shape up.

00:14:12.019 --> 00:14:13.019
Nat: Yeah, interesting.

00:14:13.019 --> 00:14:20.860
So we've had actually the absolute polar opposite
of that as far as sort of the dominant K-12

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:22.540
theme over the past decade.

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:23.540
Bryan: College for all.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:24.540
Nat: College for all, right.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:28.689
And we should design our education system
to get everybody to graduate high school and

00:14:28.689 --> 00:14:30.320
everybody to go to college.

00:14:30.320 --> 00:14:31.680
Bryan: It sounds great.

00:14:31.680 --> 00:14:32.819
Nat: How does this play?

00:14:32.819 --> 00:14:33.939
I mean, how does this play out?

00:14:33.939 --> 00:14:35.110
How do you see this playing out?

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:38.379
If we were to succeed in that, what would
we have?

00:14:38.379 --> 00:14:41.220
Bryan: If we were actually to get every kid
to graduate from college, you would need a

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.149
college degree to be a janitor.

00:14:43.149 --> 00:14:45.040
You would need a college degree to be a waiter.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:49.300
And it would no longer be in any way impressive
to employers, and so employers would then

00:14:49.300 --> 00:14:52.889
say you need to have a master's degree, or
a PhD or a professional degree to be worthy

00:14:52.889 --> 00:14:54.889
of even being our intern, right?

00:14:54.889 --> 00:14:59.139
So, I mean, you know, like, in a world where
everyone has a college degree, then garbage

00:14:59.139 --> 00:15:04.399
men have college degrees, then, you know,
janitors have college degrees, this is a crazy

00:15:04.399 --> 00:15:05.399
system.

00:15:05.399 --> 00:15:08.069
You know, it's never going to happen and we're
not even...we're nowhere close to it.

00:15:08.069 --> 00:15:09.470
Just because even though we...

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:15.869
About 70% of graduating seniors go on to college,
but the actual rate of completion is much

00:15:15.869 --> 00:15:16.869
lower.

00:15:16.869 --> 00:15:17.869
Nat: Yeah, right.

00:15:17.869 --> 00:15:21.850
Absolutely, I mean, you know, even two-year
college we're having two-thirds of our kids

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:23.239
not making it through.

00:15:23.239 --> 00:15:25.640
You know, and there certainly is a lot of
ways...

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:27.199
Let me ask...let me push back on a couple
of things.

00:15:27.199 --> 00:15:28.199
Bryan: Sure thing.

00:15:28.199 --> 00:15:32.239
Nat: So when I was in graduate school, I learned
hierarchical linear modeling.

00:15:32.239 --> 00:15:36.790
I'm just gonna use it as an example because
I TA'd it for several years, I taught it as

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:37.790
an assistant.

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:43.290
And, man, you know, I really knew every different
way to run it.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:49.779
Today, I need to go back to my textbooks and
sort of review some of my notes and sort of

00:15:49.779 --> 00:15:50.810
brush up on that.

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:54.070
But in the book you use lots of examples that
are sort of akin to that.

00:15:54.070 --> 00:16:00.799
Now I'd say, well, I would not be able to
go back and do HLM today without some review,

00:16:00.799 --> 00:16:02.999
but I can go back and do it.

00:16:02.999 --> 00:16:08.430
So in the book you use examples like, well,
a lot of kids could never pass an algebra

00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:09.559
test now.

00:16:09.559 --> 00:16:10.850
And my question is...

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:12.369
Bryan: Or adults, yeah.

00:16:12.369 --> 00:16:14.329
Nat: Or, yeah, adults.

00:16:14.329 --> 00:16:16.299
The test they could have passed when they
were kids.

00:16:16.299 --> 00:16:20.970
So my question is, isn't there some value
there that isn't being captured when you say,

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:25.589
well, we've lost the discrete skills we had
in tenth grade?

00:16:25.589 --> 00:16:27.259
Bryan: There's a small amount of value.

00:16:27.259 --> 00:16:31.790
So it's definitely true, any educational psychologist
will tell you it's easier to re-learn than

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:33.889
to learn the first time around.

00:16:33.889 --> 00:16:37.060
But so much of what people forget is stuff
they never use.

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:40.619
It never comes up in adult life, and that's
why they forget it, actually.

00:16:40.619 --> 00:16:44.660
If you were...if you periodically brush up
and refresh your memory, then at least you

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:46.089
retain the basics.

00:16:46.089 --> 00:16:52.350
And yes, it remains useful, but stuff I'm
talking about is really stuff that, really,

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:53.720
adults virtually never use.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:17:00.359
But what's striking to me is that out of adults
who actually went on to calculus as high school

00:17:00.359 --> 00:17:03.790
students, only about 20% even use algebra
on the job right now.

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:09.240
So, you know, it's not like that we're training
people something so that they can re-learn

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:11.069
it at each point where it becomes appropriate.

00:17:11.069 --> 00:17:14.740
Most of it is just stuff that you will never
encounter again after the final exam.

00:17:14.740 --> 00:17:19.039
That's why we forget it so readily is that
we have no additional practice because what

00:17:19.039 --> 00:17:23.059
was taught was not really well-thought-out
preparation for adult life.

00:17:23.059 --> 00:17:30.070
Nat: So getting even past sort of the discrete
skills that you can re-learn, how about things

00:17:30.070 --> 00:17:33.010
that can't even be sort of communicated in
the skills?

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:37.010
For instance, you know, some people will say,
well, you know, part of getting through high

00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:39.730
school is just learning how to negotiate the
system.

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:45.500
And part of the problem with kids who drop
out is they're never gonna learn to negotiate

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:50.029
the kinds of systems that are gonna give them
success of any kind in the labor market.

00:17:50.029 --> 00:17:52.900
So without, you know...

00:17:52.900 --> 00:17:59.909
If we roll back the education system substantially,
are we prohibiting kids, are we making it

00:17:59.909 --> 00:18:04.440
very hard for kids to learn how to negotiate
those systems?

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:08.740
Bryan: Right, so, this is a classic compared-to-what
question.

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:12.740
If it's either school or play video games
home alone in your basement, then school is

00:18:12.740 --> 00:18:16.690
a great socializer and I'll say nothing against
it.

00:18:16.690 --> 00:18:19.059
However, is that really the relevant alternative?

00:18:19.059 --> 00:18:22.310
In all other human societies, and earlier
in the U.S. history, the alternative to school

00:18:22.310 --> 00:18:25.399
wasn't sitting home alone, the alternative
was work.

00:18:25.399 --> 00:18:29.529
And work also teaches people to negotiate
systems, and you know, it teaches social skills,

00:18:29.529 --> 00:18:31.320
it teaches you how to get along with other
people.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:35.190
But it also teaches a bunch of social skills
that aren't really being taught very well

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:38.639
in schools today, like accepting criticism.

00:18:38.639 --> 00:18:39.639
Right?

00:18:39.639 --> 00:18:43.980
In modern schools they're so touchy-feely
and they're so positive that kids often are

00:18:43.980 --> 00:18:46.230
not told, "By the way, you screwed up, you
are not...

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:49.559
You know, your work is inadequate and you
need to improve, sorry."

00:18:49.559 --> 00:18:50.559
Right?

00:18:50.559 --> 00:18:53.100
On the job you still get that kind of thing
right now, you know.

00:18:53.100 --> 00:18:57.389
So on the job you learn the idea of, you have
to make the customer happy, right?

00:18:57.389 --> 00:18:58.870
And life is not equal or fair.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:19:00.730
These are lessons that are taught on every
job.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:05.049
School, on the other hand, you really are
preparing them for this strange world that

00:19:05.049 --> 00:19:07.730
no adult experiences of equality and fairness.

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:12.230
So, you know, better to go and prepare kids
for the world they're actually gonna be facing.

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:17.360
You know, of course, don't go and give them
immediate shock therapy where you give them

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:23.570
a boss who screams at them on the first day,
but just to move them gradually to a realistic

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:25.961
environment where people don't love you, right?

00:19:25.961 --> 00:19:28.990
You know, so like, teachers at least pretend
to love the students.

00:19:28.990 --> 00:19:32.160
I mean, even at the high school level, like,
they're so nice.

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:35.430
But in terms of preparing people for adult
life, people aren't gonna be that nice afterwards,

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:36.960
so you better get used to it.

00:19:36.960 --> 00:19:38.200
Nat: Hey everyone.

00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:42.110
Thanks for watching part one of our discussion
with Bryan Caplan.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:46.250
Part two picks up right where we left off
here, so click the link to see the next part

00:19:46.250 --> 00:19:46.859
of the series.

